name w1-10-graduation-1
timeout 700
time 2022-08-17T07:00:00Z

# CAMPAIGN W1, SORTIE 10 OF 10 - THE GRADUATION RIDE: THE WHOLE LADDER AT ONCE, AT NIGHT.
# Spec, anchor and sources: doc/campaigns/w1-red-flag.md. Format: doc/missions/campaign.md.
# CONTROL: none. THIS FILE IS THE SECOND STEP OF THE CAMPAIGN'S ONE CARRY CHAIN. `nvblu1..4`,
# `nvagr1..4` and the four range objects come out of w1-09-lfe-four.fbm: whoever was destroyed there is
# DROPPED from this file by the campaign layer (kill removal, the anchor's own range function), and every
# `set store` line whose stock ran out there is dropped too. The `campaign CARRY` lines in this step's
# `events.log` are the audit trail, and the standalone re-run of this step with step 9's state file is
# what prices the whole chain.
#
# THE CLOCK IS DECLARED AND IT IS THE ONLY FILE IN W1 THAT DECLARES ONE. 2022-08-17T07:00Z is midnight
# local at Nellis (PDT = UTC-7); the campaign's own clock is 17:00Z on the 16th, i.e. 10:00 local, and the
# nine day rides take it. Both instants are [SET] - the fact sheet's data is "current as of October 2022"
# and no exercise date is sourced. The runner logs the computed sun elevation at MISSION_START, so the
# claim "this is night" is checkable in the output without trusting this comment.
# AND NIGHT COSTS BOTH SIDES THEIR EYES, WHICH IS A LIMIT ON THE CLAIM AND NOT ON THE MECHANISM: nothing
# in this tree emits light, so `sensors/FBVisualSystem` contributes nothing after dark and every detection
# in this file is a radar or an infrared one. W5 measured what that is worth on an identification pass - 0
# `vis` lines against 9, 6 of 184 telemetry columns moved, and the same verdict at the same tick.
#
# THE ARENA is w1-01's lane, `--elev const`, 0 m range floor. Red's array is at the west end (the four
# objects of the chain), Blue's own asset at the east end. Every coordinate [SET].
#
# THE ONE TACTICAL QUESTION: does the package hold together - strike on target, escort on the threat axis,
# nobody engaged twice - when BOTH sides have a job on the ground as well as in the air?
#
# WHAT THIS FILE CAN AND CANNOT STAGE (W3's rule, stated before the result). It stages four things: a
# two-flight Blue package, a defended Blue asset, an aggressor CAP and an aggressor strike element. It
# does NOT stage the fifth thing the anchor's own LFE has, and the reason is C15: there is no
# time-on-target, no deconfliction, no lead tasking and no rejoin, so "the package holds together" can only
# be read off what each element did in ITS OWN file order - never off a plan they shared.
#
# THE TIME SEPARATION IS THE FILE'S LOAD-BEARING GEOMETRY (O1's rule 6). The run ends at the FIRST
# flight-monitor K.O. and a mission-killed jet falls for 50-120 s, so both ground jobs must be complete
# before the first body reaches the desert. The arithmetic, checkable against the spawns: Blue's strikers
# enter 25.7-30.0 km from their aim points and reach them at 231 m/s in about 111-130 s; Red's strike pair
# enters 28.2 km from Blue's asset and reaches it in about 122 s; the escorts and the aggressor CAP are
# 69.1 km apart closing at about 480 m/s, so the first Rtr shot cannot fall before about 105 s and the
# resulting ground contact not before about 170 s.
#
# THE FOURTH STRIKER IS AIMED AT THE HARDENED OBJECT ON PURPOSE, and it is the one measurement in W1 that
# is about a weapon rather than about a decision: a Mk-84's lethal radius against a `target_hard` is
# 17.7 m (doc/air-to-ground.md) and this delivery's measured error is ~22 m (doc/missions/weapons.md), so
# the bunker is INSIDE the store's envelope and OUTSIDE the delivery's. Three strikers aim at soft objects
# where the same store has 100 m; the fourth asks whether the ladder's top rung can crack a bunker.
#
# MEASURED (2026-07-30, campaign step 10 - fingerprint a9ad699a4e298df5, and the standalone replay with
# step 9's state file MATCHES it): exit 3, and the package half-holds.
#   the clock    `mission CLOCK utc=2022-08-17T07:00:00Z sunElDeg=**-38.088** moonElDeg=18.31
#                moonPhase=0.705`. It is night, checked by the runner and not by this comment. The nine
#                day rides log sunElDeg **+45.71**
#   the carry    **7 `campaign CARRY` lines**: `nvagr4` **action=drop** ("destroyed in an earlier
#                mission") - kill removal, performed by the campaign layer - plus SIX `set store` lines
#                deleted from the three surviving aggressors (nvagr1 2x r27r + 1x r73, nvagr2 2x r27r,
#                nvagr3 1x r27r)
#   the strike   three strikers released at t = 81.5 / 88.0 / 96.5 s; `aimErrM` **47.00 / 47.67 /
#                47.56 m**, of which `aimAcrossM` **44.46 / 46.03 / 47.22 m** against w1-06's 7.25 m in
#                calm air - THE FIXTURE'S WIND COSTS ~40 m OF CROSS-TRACK, which is W4's finding on a
#                second airframe-weapon pair. All three aim points DESTROYED (t = 116.3 / 123.9 / 131.9)
#                because a Mk-84's radius against a `target_soft` is 100 m
#   the bunker   `nvbnkr` INTACT - AND NOT BECAUSE THE BOMB MISSED. `nvblu4` was killed at t = 106.3 s by
#                an R-27R at **1.788 m**, before its release point. The 17.7 m question this file was
#                built to ask is NOT answered, and saying so is the answer that is available
#   the aggressor strike `aimErrM` **88.22 m** and **97.46 m** against Blue's asset - the same standing
#                cross-track offset as w1-05, and `nvasset` INTACT with the escorts' `protect` met
#   losses       Blue 1 (`nvblu4`), Red 0. Blue destroyed 3 of its 4 aim points and held its own asset
#   the time order first air-to-air kill t = 106.3 s, last Blue delivery t = 131.9 s, last aggressor
#                delivery t = 133.9 s: BOTH ground jobs completed after the first kill and before its
#                ground contact, which is why they are in the record at all (O1's rule 6)
# THE CARRY, PRICED, AND IT IS THE VISIBLE TRANSFER OF THIS CAMPAIGN. Run standalone with no state at all,
# the same file loses **TWO** Blue aircraft instead of one: `nvagr4` flies with a full magazine and kills
# `rf10esc2`. In-campaign vs standalone: **1 of 17 common telemetry files byte-identical**, 30 files
# against 32, the run **246.8 s against 175.2 s**, and the aggressors' own traces differ in **90 / 62 / 88
# of 184 columns**. Same three aim points destroyed, same bunker, same asset - THE CARRY MOVES THE AIR
# RESULT AND NOT THE GROUND ONE. Attributed with `--carry` one fact at a time: `units` alone (nvagr4
# dropped, magazines full) -> 1 Blue loss; `stores` alone (nvagr4 flies, 10 store lines dropped) -> 1 Blue
# loss; `ground` alone (neither) -> 2. EITHER HALF ALONE IS ENOUGH, so the fourth aggressor is only lethal
# with a full rack.
#
# WHAT TO READ, in this order: the `campaign CARRY` lines (who was dropped, which store lines went), then
# `pilot ATTACK_RELEASE` / `stores DELIVERY aimErrM` per Blue striker and per Red attacker,
# `mission OBJECTIVE unit=rf10esc* kind="protect unit nvasset"`, the `flt_*` block on both four-ship
# flights, and finally the TIME ORDER of the last `stores IMPACT` against the first `damage KILL`.
#
# READING RULE: exit 3 = TIMEOUT is the expected shape; exit 1 means somebody's `protect` or `survive` was
# violated and the log names whom. The verdict of the graduation ride is the QUADRUPLE (Red aim points
# destroyed, Blue's asset alive or not, escorts' protect state, aircraft lost per side) plus the
# standalone-versus-in-campaign diff that prices the carry. exit 2 would be a defect.

wx fixture wx-2026-07-27T00Z.wxb

